    "accordion_X9KDBB": {
      "type": "accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "text_8HeDjV": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is Texas tap water safe to drink?",
            "answer": "<p>This is the question more residents are asking each year. Independent tests have identified 207 contaminants in Texas water, some of which including TTHMs, arsenic, PFAS, lead  and nitrates are above more stringent standards for health, recommended by independent scientists. Therefore, legal compliance is not equal to safety.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_QbTBtT": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How serious is PFAS in Texas water? ",
            "answer": "<p><strong>PFAS in Texas water</strong> has been detected in several cities around the state, with Dallas failing water tests with high levels of PFOA and PFOS at the Bachman Water Treatment Plant, exceeding the EPA's 2024 legal limits. These \"forever chemicals\" do not degrade in the environment or the human body and have been linked to cancer, thyroid disease and developmental problems in children. Texas's Attorney General has intervened in a multi state lawsuit against these PFAS producers.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_XbcVQV": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is arsenic in Texas water a real concern? ",
            "answer": "<p>Yes - <strong>arsenic in Texas water</strong> is a particular concern for rural Texan and private well residents, since it naturally leaches from rock formations into groundwater. An assessment in 2025 identified a number of Texas groundwater systems that contained arsenic levels that exceeded health-based recommendations. Arsenic in water has no odor, taste or color so the only way to know if it is present in your water is through testing.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_NXHddK": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why choose a stainless steel water filter for a Texas home? ",
            "answer": "<p>Plastic filter housings will break down with the extreme heat in Texas and leach microplastics  into your water that you are trying to purify so stainless steel is the wiser choice.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_yUeHba": {
          "type": "text",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "question": "How often do I need to replace the Carbon filter elements?",
            "answer": "<p>One pair of Phoenix Gravity Carbon filter elements is good for up to 5,500 gallons of water. So for most homes in Miami, you will be enjoying many years of fresh water at a price of less than 5 cents per gallon.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_fE9Y4D": {
          "type": "text",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a plumber or any installation for Phoenix Gravity Filters?",
            "answer": "<p>There is no need for a plumber because of the tool-free gravity design of the system; there is no installation required and no power needed. The system offers the easiest solution to keep your Miami tap water safe from lead and microplastics and provide you with clean drinking water.</p>"
          }
        },
        "text_XzRedf": {
          "type": "text",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why is chloramine used in New York water?",
            "answer": "<p>The main reason chloramines are added to NY water is because they provide protection for the water by serving as a disinfectant while being transported through pipes to customers. Chloramines can leave a particular flavor in the water therefore, they may need to be filtered to improve their quality. </p>"
          }
        },
        "text_bVMT6E": {
          "type": "text",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is chromium 6 in New York water and should I worry?",
            "answer": "<p>Chromium (VI) is a toxic heavy metal that is found in trace amounts in NY water and is associated with industrial pollution. The greatest worry is for long-term exposure; therefore, any kind of effective filtration is very important. </p>"
          }
        }
      },
